roup 1.\n- `\\s+` matches the whitespace.\n- `\\1` must match exactly what group 1 captured.\n\nAgainst `\"this is is a test\"`, it matches `is is`. It won't match `is it`, because `\\1` demands the literal text `is`, not just any word.\n\nThis is different from repeating the group's pattern (`(\\w+)\\s+(\\w+)`), which would match any two words. A backreference matches the same *text*, not the same *pattern*.\n\nNote: backreferences make a regex non-regular in the formal sense, so some engines (e.g., RE2, Rust's `regex`) don't support them. Named groups (`(?<word>\\w+)` with `\\k<word>`) are available in many flavors for readability.","ttft_ms":3327,"type":"result","duration_ms":3358,"uuid":"850513e5-4e60-48c5-bb59-cdd298b46eb3","ttft_stream_ms":686,"time_to_request_ms":118,"first_content_frame_ms":686,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":349,"cacheReadInputTokens":4966,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0039906,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-12T05:14:51.381Z"}